3.4.m:
In accordance with the duty of varnas, they all embodied the varnas in order.
Then five hundred daughters were born from Dakṣa’s mind;
by the power of Viṣṇu’s Māyā, they remained on earth as portions [of her].

on the charming mountain Himālaya, he became the son of Sādyakarman.
Possessing a lordly form, expert in the arts, he was known as Bhāratīśa.
Having defeated multitudes of scholars, he came to the city of Kāśī;

Having approached Śaṅkarācārya and become his disciple, that Bhairava
composed the Tantra named Ḍāmara, consisting of mantras;
and the mantras that had been locked, he unlocked.

Twenty-seven four-age cycles passed while Ilā was in deep meditation;
then the maiden Ilā, taking Budha, the son of the Moon, as her husband in the Tretā age,
gave birth to the Lunar dynasty.

Hearing this, Dhanvantari became a disciple of that guru,
and dwelling there, he composed the Kalpaveda, an auxiliary of the Veda;
and other disciples of Dhanvantari besides Suśruta are remembered.
